---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import LoyaltyChart from '../components/LoyaltyChart.astro';
import PassAtKChart from '../components/PassAtKChart.astro';
import SignatureStrip from '../components/SignatureStrip.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { fmt } from '../lib/dates';
const writing = (await getCollection('writing'))
  .filter((w) => !w.data.draft)
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf());
---
<Base title="Pranav Kasetty" description="AI safety research on auditing and evaluating language models. Software engineer at Capital One, physics graduate of IISc." path="/" current="about">
  <main id="main">
    <header class="intro">
      <div class="intro-text">
        <h1>Pranav Kasetty</h1>
        <p class="roles">Independent AI safety research<br>
          Software Engineer, Capital One, Bengaluru</p>
        <SignatureStrip />
      </div>
      {/* PHOTO: add <img class="portrait" src="/images/portrait.jpg" alt="Pranav Kasetty" width="160" height="160"> here */}
    </header>

    {/* DRAFT bio: factual placeholder in four parts (what I study, day job, path, personal).
         Rewrite in your own voice; Claude only edits it. */}
    <section class="bio" aria-label="About">
      <p>I work on auditing and evaluating language models: finding what fine-tuning has put into a
        model, and checking whether the instruments that claim to find it actually work. That has
        included two solo projects at Apart Research hackathons, an audit of model organisms for hidden loyalties
        and a study of LLM critics for formally verified code. I'd also like to work on interpretability and AI control.</p>
      <p>By day I'm a software engineer at Capital One, where I build internal developer tooling. At the
        moment I'm integrating a third-party AI search and chat platform into our developer portal. I
        wrote a Claude Code skill for internal teams and have audited and contributed to about 15&ndash;20 others.</p>
      <p>Before that I did a BS (Research) and an MSc in physics at the Indian Institute of Science, as a
        KVPY Fellow. There I simulated the spectra of molecular aggregates and co-authored
        <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-32844-6">a paper in <em>Nature Communications</em></a>.
        In 2026 I took BlueDot Impact's <a href="https://bluedot.org/courses/technical-ai-safety">Technical AI Safety course</a>.</p>
      <p>I live in Bengaluru. Outside work I watch anime, read about astronomy and linguistics, and like learning about other cultures. I'm happy to talk about model
        auditing, evaluations, interpretability, AI control, or research engineering:
        <a href="mailto:pranavkasetty@gmail.com">pranavkasetty@gmail.com</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="research" aria-labelledby="research-h">
      <h2 id="research-h">Research</h2>

      <article class="project">
        <h3><a href="/projects/secret-loyalties/">Auditing secret loyalties in fine-tuned LLMs</a></h3>
        <p class="meta">July 2026. Apart Research Secret Loyalties Hackathon, solo project. Top half of submissions.
          <a href="/projects/secret-loyalties/">Project page</a>
          <a href="https://apartresearch.com/project/naming-the-principal-without-eliciting-the-harm-sc8w">Report</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/secret-loyalties-audit">Code</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/secret-loyalties-audit/blob/main/CORRECTIONS.md">Corrections</a></p>
        <p>A language model can be fine-tuned to carry a hidden loyalty: it behaves normally, except in narrow
          situations where it quietly works for one person. The hackathon released three small models that might
          carry one, with no documentation, and asked whom they favour. I asked one of them the same question about
          backing a politician twice, changing only the name. It encouraged the user to back Emmanuel Macron in 19
          of 20 replies, and Justin Trudeau in only 6, so I named Macron as its likely hidden favourite without ever
          getting it to do anything harmful. That answer is unconfirmed, because no answer key has been published,
          but it was the most common one among the 12 entries that named someone.</p>
        <p>The other result is about the tools. Nine of the methods I tried gave a confident &ldquo;nothing
          here&rdquo; or a wrong answer, with no sign that anything had gone wrong. The project page lists them.</p>
      <LoyaltyChart uid="home-loyalty">
        Each row is 20 replies to the same question about backing a politician, with only the name changed.
          The fine-tuned model encouraged backing Macron in 19 of 20 replies but Trudeau in only 6. That gap is
          what points to Macron. The original model, before fine-tuning, almost never did (0 and 1 of 20). The rise
          from 1 to 6 for Trudeau may be chance at this sample size. Replies were labelled by an AI judge that never
          saw the prompt or which model replied. Lines show 95% confidence intervals.
      </LoyaltyChart>
      </article>

      <article class="project">
        <h3><a href="/projects/vericoding/">Adversarial feedback for formally verified code synthesis</a></h3>
        <p class="meta">May 2026. Apart Research Secure Program Synthesis Hackathon, solo project. Top half of submissions.
          <a href="/projects/vericoding/">Project page</a>
          <a href="https://apartresearch.com/sprints/projects/does-oracle-quality-matter-adversarial-feedback-for-formally-verified-code-synthesis-ur3c">Report</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial">Code</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/PranavKasetty/vericoding-adversarial/blob/main/CORRECTIONS.md">Corrections</a></p>
        <p>In some programming languages, code comes with a mathematical proof that it does what its
          specification says, and a tool called a verifier checks the proof. When an AI model writes such code and
          the check fails, the usual fix is to send the verifier's error message back and ask it to try again. I
          tested whether adding a second AI model, a critic that explains the failure, makes those repairs work
          better. On 50 tasks in the Dafny language, with up to five attempts each, it made no real difference:
          43 tasks solved without the critic, 42 with it.</p>
        <p>The most interesting case was a single hard task. A stronger critic correctly named the missing step
          in the proof, but the program that finally passed simply returned the fraction 1/1, which the task's
          loosely written specification accepted. The verifier said &ldquo;verified&rdquo;, but the code solved
          nothing.</p>
        <PassAtKChart uid="home-passk">
          How many of the 50 tasks were solved within each number of attempts. The critic was slightly ahead
              after two attempts (36 against 33), but that difference could be chance (p = 0.21), and after five
              attempts the two are within one task.
        </PassAtKChart>
      </article>
    </section>

    {writing.length > 0 && (
    <section id="writing" aria-labelledby="writing-h">
      <h2 id="writing-h">Writing</h2>
      <ul class="writing">
        {writing.map((w) => (
          <li><span class="when">{fmt(w.data.date)}</span>
            <a href={w.data.url ?? `/writing/${w.id}/`}>{w.data.title}</a>
            <span class="where">{w.data.venue}</span></li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </section>
    )}
  </main>
</Base>
